<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romance of the Western Chamber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1300</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Xixiang ji, The West Chamber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Romance of the Western Chamber (Xixiang ji) is a Chinese love story best known through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaju">zaju</a> drama by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shifu">Wang Shifu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. It tells of the scholar Zhang Sheng and Cui Yingying, who meet in a monastery and, helped by the maid Hongniang, defy her mother. Derived from a Tang tale by Yuan Zhen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-jieyuan">Dong Jieyuan</a>'s Jin version, it became one of the most popular plays in Chinese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber.jpg" alt="Romance of the Western Chamber" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 闵齐伋 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%97%B5%E9%BD%90%E4%BC%8B%E7%BB%98%E5%88%BB%E8%A5%BF%E5%8E%A2%E8%AE%B0%E5%BD%A9%E5%9B%BE%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
